{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>For it was near.</b> That is, the people near to the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said: 'the children of Israel, the people near Him.' And therefore He did not lead them in the customary way of the world. Another explanation: for the people of the Philistines are near to Egypt, as it is said: 'and Mitzrayim begat,' etc., 'from there the Philistines went forth.'" "<b>Lest the people be grieved.</b> That they would fight against them on account of the fact that their relatives had fled from under the hand of Egypt, and they would return to Egypt, and he would bring them back to Egypt. The Rabbi David, grandfather of the Rabbi Moses, said that the land of the Philistines was part of the land of Israel, for we do not find that Yitzhak went outside the land and dwelt in the land of the Philistines, as it is said: 'and Yitzhak dwelt in Gerar.' And the Rabbi Moses said that a strip of land went out from the land of the Philistines and entered into the land of Israel, and not that the land of Israel itself was so, for the verse calls it the land of the Philistines. Another explanation: 'for it was near' — because the Canaanites had inherited the land, as it is written: 'and the fourth generation shall return here,' and they did not yet have a fourth generation; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, encircled them in the wilderness for forty years. And furthermore, the Holy One, blessed be He, said: if I lead them by the direct route, now each man holds on to his field and his vineyard and neglects the study of Torah; but I will lead them forty years through the wilderness, and they will eat the manna and drink from the well, and the Torah will be settled in their bodies. And furthermore, when the Canaanites heard that Israel had gone out of Egypt, they said: now they are coming upon us and taking the land from our hands. They arose and destroyed the land. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I promised Avraham to bring his sons into a land full of all good — behold, I am holding them back in the wilderness so that they may repair what they had spoiled. As it is said: 'I instructed you in the wilderness to strengthen their arms,' etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:12.177605059Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}